# Who may touch which topics on the Weasley Clock's broker.
#
# Installed to /etc/mosquitto/weasley.acl by deploy/mosquitto-setup.sh.
#
# The point of splitting the roles is the display nodes. Six ESP32 boards sit
# in the cabinet on the house WiFi, each with its MQTT password in a plain
# config.json on its own flash. Anyone who can unscrew a bezel can read it. If
# that password were the controller's, it would let them publish fake
# positions into clock/location/<person> and move every hand on the clock.
# With the roles split, the worst it does is let them repaint their own
# display.
#
# The topic tree is in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and is the Pico firmware's and the
# node firmware's contract as much as the controller's, so it is not changed
# here -- only restricted.

# -- the controller -------------------------------------------------------
# Owns the whole clock/ tree: publishes state, display and system topics,
# subscribes to clock/location/<person> for the generic source.
user clock
topic readwrite clock/#
# OwnTracks publishes outside the clock/ tree. Read-only: the controller has
# no business writing to a phone's topic.
topic read owntracks/#

# -- the six display nodes ------------------------------------------------
# Each node subscribes to its own clock/display/<person> and publishes its own
# clock/node/<person>/status.
#
# These are "+" rather than per-node patterns because Mosquitto can only match
# a pattern against the username or the client id, and the nodes' client id is
# weasley-<person>-<mac> while the topic carries the bare <person>. So one node
# can in principle read another's display topic and write another's status.
# That is a repaint and a wrong status line, by someone who already has
# physical access to the cabinet -- a long way short of moving the hands.
user display
topic read clock/display/+
# So a node can grey itself out when the controller goes away (Last Will).
topic read clock/system/status
topic write clock/node/+/status

# -- phones, if OwnTracks is ever used ------------------------------------
# Not created by default: deploy/mosquitto-setup.sh --with-phones adds it.
# Write-only, and only into the OwnTracks tree -- a phone never needs to read
# anything from this broker, and should not be able to see where anyone else
# is.
user phone
topic write owntracks/#

# No `pattern` or `topic` lines outside a `user` block: an unmatched client
# gets no access at all, which is what we want for anything unexpected.
